= Compositionality — Simple =

'''Compositionality''' is the idea that the meaning of a larger expression depends on the meanings of its parts and how those parts are put together.

“The dog chased the cat” and “The cat chased the dog” use almost the same words, but their structure changes the meaning.

That is why compositionality needs both word meaning and [[Syntax/Simple|syntax]].

The idea helps explain how people understand new sentences they have never heard before.

It is not perfect word-by-word replacement. Idioms, context, ambiguous words, and nonliteral language can make interpretation more complicated.

